<WikiLayout title="WARDOGS Wiki Advertising Policy" description="How advertising, commercial disclosures and editorial independence are handled on WARDOGS Wiki." canonicalPath="/advertising-policy/" pageType="WebPage" primaryKeyword="WARDOGS Wiki advertising policy">
  <h1>WARDOGS Wiki Advertising Policy</h1>
  <p>This page explains how advertising can support WARDOGS Wiki while remaining separate from database records, guides, comparisons and editorial decisions.</p>
  <h2>Advertising disclosure</h2><p>Paid placements are labeled as advertisements and are rendered outside the editorial article. An advertisement does not indicate that WARDOGS Wiki endorses the advertiser, product or claim shown inside it.</p>
  <h2>Editorial independence</h2><p>Advertising configuration does not change rankings, entity relationships, comparison outcomes or the treatment of unconfirmed information. Pages continue to follow the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> and <a href="/sources/">source standards</a>.</p>
  <h2>Performance and placement</h2><p>Advertising scripts load only when a configured placement approaches the viewport. Space is reserved before loading to reduce layout movement, and unconfigured placements are not shown.</p>
  <h2>Authorized sellers</h2><p>The machine-readable <a href="/ads.txt">ads.txt file</a> lists authorized advertising sellers when publisher records have been configured. The project does not publish invented seller or account identifiers.</p>
  <h2>Third-party services</h2><p>An advertising provider may process technical request data according to its own policies when an advertisement is loaded. Deployment operators are responsible for configuring any consent or regional compliance controls required for their audience.</p>
  <h2>Contact and corrections</h2><p>Misleading, unsafe or incorrectly labeled advertising should be reported through the project repository so the placement can be reviewed without altering the surrounding Wiki content.</p>
</WikiLayout>
